This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:

   This conversation is a continuation of ongoing work building out Jenkins-based CI for the Gkeyll plasma-physics codebase (repo `gkeyllorg/gkeyll`, currently on branch `agent_tools-jenkins`), triggered by PRs into `main`, running on the user's own hardware. Explicit requests made in this portion of the conversation, in order:

   a. (Confirmation carried over from prior context, then implemented in this session) Support multiple independent Jenkins controllers — each person should be able to run their own fully independent Jenkins controller (own install, own GitHub credential, own multibranch job) without needing SSH access into another controller's machine, because "other users should be able to have their own independent controller, i.e. they may not want to give my controller SSH access to their node."

   b. Clarifying question (answered, no changes needed): confirm that adding a remote team node/workstation via SSH under an *existing* controller is still supported and documented — yes, unaffected by (a).

   c. "I think the introductory paragraphs in the README should change, because it talks about 'this laptop', but another user reading this README for the first time will be confused because they may be reading it from another computer (not necessarily a laptop)" — fixed directly.

   d. "One more edit: in a few places the instructions assume that one is building on a Mac (because it installs several things with brew). In each of these places, can you add a distinction between the steps needed on a Mac and those needed on a Linux machine, if they are different?" — fixed directly.

   e. **(Current, unfinished task)** "At the moment Jenkins CI is only running the unit tests. Gkeyll also has a Lua tool, runregression (gkeyll/lua/Tool/runregression.lua, and see ../gkylsoft/gkeyll/bin/gkeyll runregression -h), to run regression tests. Can you advise on or create a plan for adjusting Jenkins CI so that it also runs runregression?" — this required re-entering Plan Mode, researching `runregression.lua` thoroughly, and designing an approach through two rounds of user clarification. The user has now made two explicit design decisions (see below) but no plan has been finalized or approved yet.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Jenkins scripted pipeline (`ci/jenkins/Jenkinsfile`), `node(label) { ... }` blocks inside `parallel()`.
   - `CI_OWNED_NODE_LABELS`: new controller-level global Jenkins environment variable (set via Manage Jenkins → System → Global properties → Environment variables) that scopes a given controller to only the subset of the shared `nodes` map it actually owns agents for; fails the build fast via `error()` if unset.
   - Jenkins controller vs. node/agent distinction (already documented in README).
   - `gkeyll` CLI tool: a Lua-embedding executable (`gkeyll/gkeyll.c`, `gkeyll/Makefile-gkeyll`) installed to `<prefix>/gkeyll/bin/gkeyll`, which also ships a Lua standard library including `gkeyll/lua/Tool/runregression.lua`.
   - `runregression.lua`: a self-contained Lua CLI tool (via `Lib.argparse`) run as `gkeyll runregression <command> ...`. Discovers Lua tests at `<layer>/luareg/rt_*.lua` and C tests at `<layer>/creg/rt_*.c` for layers `moments`, `vlasov`, `gyrokinetic`, `pkpm` (NOT `core`). Requires SQLite3 (`GKYL_HAVE_SQLITE3`, bundled via `core/minus/sqlite3.c`). Requires `make install` (specifically the `gkeyll-install` chain) to have been run first, since C-test compilation (`compileCTest`) needs the **installed** `<prefix>/gkeyll/share/Makefile`.
   - `runregression` commands: `configure -s/--source-dir <path> [-p/--prefix] [-m/--mpiexec] [--drop-tables]`; `list [...]`; `run [layer] [check|create] [-r/--run-only] [-a/--all] [-m/--moat] [-c/--c-only] [-l/--lua-only] [-t/--timeout SECS] [--gpu-tol] [--no-gpu] [-j/--jobs N]`; `listunit`/`rununit`.
   - **MOAT ("Mother Of All Tests")**: an existing curated regression-test subset (`<layer>/luareg/moat_lua.lua`, `<layer>/creg/moat_c.lua` for the 4 layers), selected via `--moat`. Total 34 tests (17 Lua + 17 C: moments 12, vlasov 8, gyrokinetic 6, pkpm 8) — identified as the right default subset for CI given the full corpus is ~748 files (~575 after ignore-list exclusions).
   - Per-layer **ignore lists** (`ignore_lua_tests.lua`/`ignore_c_tests.lua`) already exist and are applied by default (bypassed only with `--all`).
   - `compareFiles`/tolerance logic: diffs `.gkyl` output files via the `G0.Zero` Lua-C API; default `absTol=relTol=1e-12` for CPU-vs-accepted comparisons (very tight — a cross-machine reproducibility risk if baselines were generated on a different machine/toolchain than the one doing the check, which is exactly why "build main first, same session" was chosen over a persistently-cached or git-committed baseline).
   - **Accepted baseline storage**: plain files on disk at `<prefix>/gkeyll-results/<layer>/{luareg,creg}-accepted/<testname>/*.gkyl`, resolved via `acceptedDir(test, testType)` (line 1308 of runregression.lua). Confirmed these can be manually copied between different prefixes' `gkeyll-results` trees, since `compareFiles` reads directly from these directories (not from the SQLite DB).
   - **`--prefix` coupling**: `runregression configure`'s `prefix` argument is validated as a directory and used BOTH to locate `<prefix>/gkeyll/share/Makefile` (for on-the-fly C test compilation) AND to determine where `<prefix>/gkeyll-results/` (accepted baselines + DB) lives — these are coupled, not independently configurable. This means the baseline location cannot be decoupled from a build's own install prefix via a flag; instead, cross-build baseline sharing must be done by literally copying files between two separate prefixes' `gkeyll-results` trees.
   - **SQLite schema** (from `runregression.lua` lines ~197-249): `RegressionMeta` (guid, tstamp, GKYL_EXEC, GKYL_GIT_CHANGESET, GKYL_BUILD_DATE, ntotal, npass, nfail, gpu_build, ngpu_pass, ngpu_fail) and `RegressionData` (guid, name, test_type, status, runtime, runlog, gpu_status, gpu_runtime, cpu_gpu_diff), status codes: `-4`=compile_fail, `-3`=timeout, `-2`=create, `-1`=skip, `0`=fail, `1`=pass. DB is opened via `local sql = require "sqlite3"` then `sql.open(dbPath)` (standard Lua sqlite3 binding), one DB per layer at `<resultsDir>/<layer>/regressiondb`.
   - **Critical exit-code gap**: `runregression run` never calls `os.exit()` on test failures — the process always exits 0 unless there's a configuration error (missing `--source-dir`, unknown `--run-only` name, etc.). This means Jenkins cannot rely on the shell command's exit code to detect regression failures; it must separately query the per-layer `regressiondb` (or parse output) and explicitly fail the build itself.
   - **`make install` gap**: confirmed neither `ci/jenkins/Jenkinsfile` nor `.github/workflows/mac_build.yml` currently runs any install step. The correct target is `gkeyll-install` (chained: `core-install` ← `moments-install` ← `vlasov-install` ← `gyrokinetic-install` ← `pkpm-install` ← `gkeyll-install`, top-level Makefile), which also generates `<prefix>/gkeyll/share/config.mak` and `<prefix>/gkeyll/share/Makefile` needed by runregression.
   - **`make regression` vs. `runregression.lua`**: confirmed these are two wholly separate, unrelated systems that happen to operate on the same underlying `<layer>/creg/rt_*.c` source files. `make regression` (already run by Jenkins today) just compiles them into standalone executables under `build/<layer>/creg/` using the in-repo dev-tree Makefiles — it doesn't run them and has no concept of Lua tests, baselines, or SQLite. `runregression.lua` independently recompiles the same C sources on-the-fly (per test, in scratch dirs) using the **installed** `share/Makefile`, and additionally runs the Lua-based tests, diffs output, and records results in SQLite. `make regression` is not a prerequisite for `runregression`.
